AMC.add({"comp": "amc12", "id": "2008A", "label": "2008 A", "problems": [{"q": "<p> A bakery owner turns on his doughnut machine at <span class=\"tex\">8:30 \\text{ AM}</span>. At <span class=\"tex\">11:10 \\text{ AM}</span> the machine has completed one third of the day's job. At what time will the doughnut machine complete the job?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">1:50 \\text{ PM}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3:00 \\text{ PM}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3: 30 \\text{ PM}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4:30 \\text{ PM}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5: 50 \\text{ PM}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">1:50 PM</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">3:00 PM</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">3: 30 \\mathrm{PM}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">4:30 PM</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">5: 50 \\mathrm{PM}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>The machine worked for 2 hours and 40 minutes, or 160 minutes, to complete one third of the job, so the entire job will take <span class=\"tex\">3 \\cdot 160=480</span> minutes, or 8 hours. Hence the doughnut machine will complete the job at <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{4:30 PM}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-1"}, {"q": "<p> What is the reciprocal of <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{2}{3}?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{6}{7}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{7}{6}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{7}{2}</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{6}{7}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{7}{6}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{3}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">3</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{7}{2}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Note that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{2}{3}=\\dfrac{3}{6}+\\dfrac{4}{6}=\\dfrac{7}{6}</span></p><p>The reciprocal of <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{7}{6}</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\dfrac{6}{7}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-2"}, {"q": "<p> Suppose that <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2}{3}</span> of <span class=\"tex\">10</span> bananas are worth as much as <span class=\"tex\">8</span> oranges. How many oranges are worth as much as <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}</span> of <span class=\"tex\">5</span> bananas?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{7}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">2</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{2}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">3</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{7}{2}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">4</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Note that <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2}{3}</span> of 10 bananas is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{20}{3}</span> bananas, which are worth as much as 8 oranges. So one banana is worth as much as <span class=\"tex\">8 \\cdot \\dfrac{3}{20}=\\dfrac{6}{5}</span> oranges. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}</span> of 5 bananas are worth as much as <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{6}{5}= \\boxed{3}</span> oranges.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-3"}, {"q": "<p> Which of the following is equal to the product</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{8}{4} \\cdot \\dfrac{12}{8} \\cdot \\dfrac{16}{12} \\cdots \\dfrac{4 n+4}{4 n} \\cdots \\cdot \\dfrac{2008}{2004} ?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">251</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">502</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1004</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2008</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4016</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">251</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">502</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">1004</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">2008</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">4016</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Because each denominator except the first can be canceled with the previous numerator, the product is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2008}{4}= \\boxed{502}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-4"}, {"q": "<p> Suppose that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{2 x}{3}-\\dfrac{x}{6}</span></p><p>is an integer. Which of the following statements must be true about <span class=\"tex\">x?</span></p>", "c": ["It is negative.", "It is even, but not necessarily a multiple of <span class=\"tex\">3</span>.", "It is a multiple of <span class=\"tex\">3</span>, but not necessarily even.", "It is a multiple of <span class=\"tex\">6</span>, but not necessarily a multiple of <span class=\"tex\">12</span>.", "It is a multiple of <span class=\"tex\">12</span> ."], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. It is negative.<br/>\nB. It is even, but not necessarily a multiple of 3 .<br/>\nC. It is a multiple of 3 , but not necessarily even.<br/>\nD. It is a multiple of 6 , but not necessarily a multiple of 12 .<br/>\nE. It is a multiple of 12 .<br/>\n</p>\n<p>Because</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{2 x}{3}-\\dfrac{x}{6}=\\dfrac{x}{2}</span></p><p>is an integer, <span class=\"tex\">x</span> must be even. The case <span class=\"tex\">x=4</span> shows that <span class=\"tex\">x</span> is not necessarily a multiple of 3 and that none of the other statements must be true.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-5"}, {"q": "<p> Heather compares the price of a new computer at two different stores. Store A offers <span class=\"tex\">15 \\%</span> off the sticker price followed by a <span class=\"tex\">\\$ 90</span> rebate, and store B offers <span class=\"tex\">25 \\%</span> off the same sticker price with no rebate. Heather saves <span class=\"tex\">\\$ 15</span> by buying the computer at store A instead of store B. What is the sticker price of the computer, in dollars?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">750</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">900</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1000</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1050</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1500</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">750</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">900</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">1000</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">1050</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">1500</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">x</span> denote the sticker price, in dollars. Heather pays <span class=\"tex\">0.85 x-90</span> dollars at store A and would have paid <span class=\"tex\">0.75 x</span> dollars at store B. Thus the sticker price <span class=\"tex\">x</span> satisfies <span class=\"tex\">0.85 x-90=0.75 x-15</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{x=750}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-6"}, {"q": "<p> While Steve and LeRoy are fishing <span class=\"tex\">1</span> mile from shore, their boat springs a leak, and water comes in at a constant rate of <span class=\"tex\">10</span> gallons per minute. The boat will sink if it takes in more than <span class=\"tex\">30</span> gallons of water. Steve starts rowing toward the shore at a constant rate of <span class=\"tex\">4</span> miles per hour while LeRoy bails water out of the boat. What is the slowest rate, in gallons per minute, at which LeRoy can bail if they are to reach the shore without sinking?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">2</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">4</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">6</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">8</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">10</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>At the rate of 4 miles per hour, Steve can row a mile in 15 minutes. During that time <span class=\"tex\">15 \\cdot 10=150</span> gallons of water will enter the boat. LeRoy must bail <span class=\"tex\">150-30=120</span> gallons of water during that time. So he must bail at the rate of at least <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{120}{15}= \\boxed{8}</span> gallons per minute.</p>\n<p>OR</p>\n<p>Steve must row for 15 minutes to reach the shore, so the amount of water in the boat can increase by at most <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{30}{15}=2</span> gallons per minute. Therefore LeRoy must bail out at least <span class=\"tex\">10-2= \\boxed{8}</span> gallons per minute.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-7"}, {"q": "<p> What is the volume of a cube whose surface area is twice that of a cube with volume <span class=\"tex\">1?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2 \\sqrt{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{2}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">2</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">2 \\sqrt{2}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">4</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">8</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">x</span> be the side length of the larger cube. The larger cube has surface area <span class=\"tex\">6 x^{2}</span>, and the smaller cube has surface area 6 . So <span class=\"tex\">6 x^{2}=2 \\cdot 6=12</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">x=\\sqrt{2}</span>. The volume of the larger cube is <span class=\"tex\">x^{3}=(\\sqrt{2})^{3}= \\boxed{2 \\sqrt{2}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-8"}, {"q": "<p> Older television screens have an aspect ratio of <span class=\"tex\">4:3</span>. That is, the ratio of the width to the height is <span class=\"tex\">4: 3</span>. The aspect ratio of many movies is not <span class=\"tex\">4: 3</span>, so they are sometimes shown on a television screen by \"letterboxing\" Â— darkening strips of equal height at the top and bottom of the screen, as shown. Suppose a movie has an aspect ratio of <span class=\"tex\">2:1</span> and is shown on an older television screen with a <span class=\"tex\">27</span>-inch diagonal. What is the height, in inches, of each darkened strip?</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2008A/2023_11_14_bb2e7c9254f50af533a3g-112.jpg\" width=\"200\"/></div>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2.25</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2.5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2.7</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">2</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">2.25</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">2.5</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">2.7</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">3</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">h</span> and <span class=\"tex\">w</span> be the height and width of the screen, respectively, in inches. By the Pythagorean Theorem, <span class=\"tex\">h: w: 27=3: 4: 5</span>, so</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">h=\\dfrac{3}{5} \\cdot 27=16.2 \\quad \\text { and } \\quad w=\\dfrac{4}{5} \\cdot 27=21.6</span></p><p>The height of the non-darkened portion of the screen is half the width, or 10.8 inches. Therefore the height of each darkened strip is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{1}{2}(16.2-10.8)= \\boxed{2.7} \\quad \\text { inches. }</span></p><p>OR</p>\n<p>The screen has dimensions <span class=\"tex\">4 a \\times 3 a</span> for some <span class=\"tex\">a</span>. The portion of the screen not covered by the darkened strips has aspect ratio <span class=\"tex\">2: 1</span>, so it has dimensions <span class=\"tex\">4 a \\times 2 a</span>. Thus the darkened strips each have height <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{a}{2}</span>. By the Pythagorean Theorem, the diagonal of the screen is <span class=\"tex\">5 a=27</span> inches. Hence the height of each darkened strip is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{27}{10}= \\boxed{2.7}</span> inches.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-9"}, {"q": "<p> Doug can paint a room in <span class=\"tex\">5</span> hours. Dave can paint the same room in <span class=\"tex\">7</span> hours. Doug and Dave paint the room together and take a one-hour break for lunch. Let <span class=\"tex\">t</span> be the total time, in hours, required for them to complete the job working together, including lunch. Which of the following equations is satisfied by <span class=\"tex\">t?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)(t+1)=1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right) t+1=1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right) t=1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)(t-1)=1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">(5+7) t=1</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)(t+1)=1</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right) t+1=1</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right) t=1</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)(t-1)=1</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">(5+7) t=1</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>In one hour Doug can paint <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{5}</span> of the room, and Dave can paint <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{7}</span> of the room. Working together, they can paint <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{1}{7}</span> of the room in one hour. It takes them <span class=\"tex\">t</span> hours to do the job, but because they take an hour for lunch, they work for only <span class=\"tex\">t-1</span> hours. The fraction of the room that they paint in this time is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\boxed{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)(t-1)}</span></p><p>which must be equal to 1 . It may be checked that the solution, <span class=\"tex\">t=\\dfrac{47}{12}</span>, does not satisfy the equation in any of the other answer choices.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-10"}, {"q": "<p> Three cubes are each formed from the pattern shown. They are then stacked on a table one on top of another so that the <span class=\"tex\">13</span> visible numbers have the greatest possible sum. What is that sum?</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2008A/2023_11_14_bb2e7c9254f50af533a3g-112_1_.jpg\" width=\"250\"/></div>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">154</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">159</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">164</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">167</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">189</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">154</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">159</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">164</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">167</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">189</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>The sum of the six numbers on each cube is <span class=\"tex\">1+2+4+8+16+32=</span> 63. The three pairs of opposite faces have numbers with sums <span class=\"tex\">1+32=33</span>, <span class=\"tex\">2+16=18</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">4+8=12</span>. On the two lower cubes, the numbers on the four visible faces have the greatest sum when the 4 and the 8 are hidden. On the top cube, the numbers on the five visible faces have the greatest sum when the 1 is hidden. Thus the greatest possible sum is <span class=\"tex\">3 \\cdot 63-2 \\cdot(4+8)-1= \\boxed{164}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-11"}, {"q": "<p> A function <span class=\"tex\">f</span> has domain <span class=\"tex\">[0,2]</span> and range <span class=\"tex\">[0,1]</span>. (The notation <span class=\"tex\">[a, b]</span> denotes <span class=\"tex\">\\{x: a \\leq x \\leq b\\}</span>.) What are the domain and range, respectively, of the function <span class=\"tex\">g</span> defined by <span class=\"tex\">g(x)=1-f(x+1)?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">[-1,1],[-1,0]</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">[-1,1],[0,1]</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">[0,2],[-1,0]</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">[1,3],[-1,0]</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">[1,3],[0,1]</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">[-1,1],[-1,0]</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">[-1,1],[0,1]</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">[0,2],[-1,0]</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">[1,3],[-1,0]</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">[1,3],[0,1]</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Because the domain of <span class=\"tex\">f</span> is <span class=\"tex\">[0,2], f(x+1)</span> is defined for <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq x+1 \\leq 2</span>, or <span class=\"tex\">-1 \\leq x \\leq 1</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">g(x)</span> is also defined for <span class=\"tex\">-1 \\leq x \\leq 1</span>, so its domain is <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{[-1,1]}</span>. Because the range of <span class=\"tex\">f</span> is <span class=\"tex\">[0,1]</span>, the values of <span class=\"tex\">f(x+1)</span> are all the numbers between 0 and 1 , inclusive. Thus the values of <span class=\"tex\">g(x)</span> are all the numbers between <span class=\"tex\">1-0=1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">1-1=0</span>, inclusive, so the range of <span class=\"tex\">g</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{[0,1]}</span>.</p>\n<p>OR</p>\n<center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2008A/2024_06_22_1fefda5e99366fb06e42g-140_2_.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>\n<p>The graph of <span class=\"tex\">y=f(x+1)</span> is obtained by shifting the graph of <span class=\"tex\">y=f(x)</span> one unit to the left. The graph of <span class=\"tex\">y=-f(x+1)</span> is obtained by reflecting the graph of <span class=\"tex\">y=f(x+1)</span> across the <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-axis. The graph of <span class=\"tex\">y=g(x)=1-f(x+1)</span> is obtained by shifting the graph of <span class=\"tex\">y=-f(x+1)</span> up one unit. As the figures illustrate, the domain and range of <span class=\"tex\">g</span> are <span class=\"tex\">[-1,1]</span> and <span class=\"tex\">[0,1]</span>, respectively.\\</p>\n<center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2008A/2024_06_22_1fefda5e99366fb06e42g-140.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-12"}, {"q": "<p> Points <span class=\"tex\">A</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B</span> lie on a circle centered at <span class=\"tex\">O</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A O B=60^{\\circ}</span>. A second circle is internally tangent to the first and tangent to both <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{O A}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{O B}</span>. What is the ratio of the area of the smaller circle to that of the larger circle?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{16}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{9}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{8}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{6}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{4}</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{16}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{9}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{8}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{6}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{4}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">r</span> and <span class=\"tex\">R</span> be the radii of the smaller and larger circles, respectively. Let <span class=\"tex\">E</span> be the center of the smaller circle, let <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{O C}</span> be the radius of the larger circle that contains <span class=\"tex\">E</span>, and let <span class=\"tex\">D</span> be the point of tangency of the smaller circle to <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{O A}</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">O E=R-r</span>, and because <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle E D O</span> is a <span class=\"tex\">30-60-90^{\\circ}</span> triangle, <span class=\"tex\">O E=2 D E=2 r</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">2 r=R-r</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{r}{R}=\\dfrac{1}{3}</span>. The ratio of the areas is</p>\n<center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2008A/2024_06_22_1fefda5e99366fb06e42g-140_1_.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>\n<p><span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{2}= \\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{9}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-13"}, {"q": "<p> What is the area of the region defined by the inequality <span class=\"tex\">|3 x-18|+|2 y+7| \\leq 3?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{7}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{9}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">3</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{7}{2}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">4</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{9}{2}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">5</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>The boundaries of the region are the two pairs of parallel lines</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(3 x-18)+(2 y+7)= \\pm 3 \\quad \\text { and } \\quad(3 x-18)-(2 y+7)= \\pm 3</span></p><p>These lines intersect at <span class=\"tex\">(6,-2),(6,-5),\\left(5,-\\dfrac{7}{2}\\right)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">\\left(7,-\\dfrac{7}{2}\\right)</span>. Thus the region is a rhombus whose diagonals have lengths 2 and 3 . The area of the rhombus is half the product of the diagonal lengths, which is <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{3}</span> .</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-14"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">k=2008^{2}+2^{2008}</span>. What is the units digit of <span class=\"tex\">k^{2}+2^{k}?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">0</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">0</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">2</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">4</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">6</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">8</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>The units digit of <span class=\"tex\">2^{n}</span> is <span class=\"tex\">2,4,8</span>, and 6 for <span class=\"tex\">n=1,2,3</span>, and 4 , respectively. For <span class=\"tex\">n&gt;4</span>, the units digit of <span class=\"tex\">2^{n}</span> is equal to that of <span class=\"tex\">2^{n-4}</span>. Thus for every positive integer <span class=\"tex\">j</span> the units digit of <span class=\"tex\">2^{4 j}</span> is 6 , and hence <span class=\"tex\">2^{2008}</span> has a units digit of 6 . The units digit of <span class=\"tex\">2008^{2}</span> is 4 . Therefore the units digit of <span class=\"tex\">k</span> is 0 , so the units digit of <span class=\"tex\">k^{2}</span> is also 0 . Because 2008 is even, both <span class=\"tex\">2008^{2}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">2^{2008}</span> are multiples of 4 . Therefore <span class=\"tex\">k</span> is a multiple of 4 , so the units digit of <span class=\"tex\">2^{k}</span> is 6 , and the units digit of <span class=\"tex\">k^{2}+2^{k}</span> is also <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{6}</span> .</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-15"}, {"q": "<p> The numbers <span class=\"tex\">\\log \\left(a^{3} b^{7}\\right), \\log \\left(a^{5} b^{12}\\right)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">\\log \\left(a^{8} b^{15}\\right)</span> are the first three terms of an arithmetic sequence, and the <span class=\"tex\">12^{\\text {th }}</span> term of the sequence is <span class=\"tex\">\\log \\left(b^{n}\\right)</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">n?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">40</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">56</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">76</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">112</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">143</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">40</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">56</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">76</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">112</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">143</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>The first three terms of the sequence can be written as <span class=\"tex\">3 \\log a+7 \\log b, 5 \\log a+12 \\log b</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">8 \\log a+15 \\log b</span>. The difference between consecutive terms can be written either as</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(5 \\log a+12 \\log b)-(3 \\log a+7 \\log b)=2 \\log a+5 \\log b</span></p><p>or as</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(8 \\log a+15 \\log b)-(5 \\log a+12 \\log b)=3 \\log a+3 \\log b</span></p><p>Thus <span class=\"tex\">\\log a=2 \\log b</span>, so the first term of the sequence is <span class=\"tex\">13 \\log b</span>, and the difference between consecutive terms is <span class=\"tex\">9 \\log b</span>. Hence the <span class=\"tex\">12^{\\text {th }}</span> term is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(13+(12-1) \\cdot 9) \\log b=112 \\log b=\\boxed{\\log \\left(b^{112}\\right)}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-16"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">a_{1}, a_{2}, \\ldots</span> be a sequence of integers determined by the rule <span class=\"tex\">a_{n}=a_{n-1} / 2</span> if <span class=\"tex\">a_{n-1}</span> is even and <span class=\"tex\">a_{n}=3 a_{n-1}+1</span> if <span class=\"tex\">a_{n-1}</span> is odd. For how many positive integers <span class=\"tex\">a_{1} \\leq 2008</span> is it true that <span class=\"tex\">a_{1}</span> is less than each of <span class=\"tex\">a_{2}, a_{3}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">a_{4}?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">250</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">251</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">501</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">502</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1004</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">250</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">251</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">501</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">502</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">1004</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>If <span class=\"tex\">a_{1}</span> is even, then <span class=\"tex\">a_{2}=\\left(a_{1} / 2\\right)&lt;a_{1}</span>, so the required condition is not met. If <span class=\"tex\">a_{1} \\equiv 1(\\bmod 4)</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">a_{2}=3 a_{1}+1</span> is a multiple of 4 , so <span class=\"tex\">a_{3}=</span> <span class=\"tex\">\\left(3 a_{1}+1\\right) / 2</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">a_{4}=\\left(3 a_{1}+1\\right) / 4 \\leq a_{1}</span>. Hence the required condition is also not met in this case. If <span class=\"tex\">a_{1} \\equiv 3(\\bmod 4)</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">a_{2}</span> is even but not a multiple of 4 . It follows that <span class=\"tex\">a_{3}=\\left(3 a_{1}+1\\right) / 2&gt;a_{1}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">a_{3}</span> is odd, so <span class=\"tex\">a_{4}=3 a_{3}+1&gt;a_{3}&gt;a_{1}</span>. Because 2008 is a multiple of 4 , a total of <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2008}{4}= \\boxed{502}</span> possible values of <span class=\"tex\">a_{1}</span> are congruent to <span class=\"tex\">3(\\bmod 4)</span>. These 502 values of <span class=\"tex\">a_{1}</span> meet the required condition.</p>\n<p>Note: It is a famous unsolved problem to show whether or not the number 1 must be a term of this sequence for every choice of <span class=\"tex\">a_{1}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-17"}, {"q": "<p> Triangle <span class=\"tex\">A B C</span>, with sides of length <span class=\"tex\">5,6</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">7</span>, has one vertex on the positive <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-axis, one on the positive <span class=\"tex\">y</span>-axis, and one on the positive <span class=\"tex\">z</span>-axis. Let <span class=\"tex\">O</span> be the origin. What is the volume of tetrahedron <span class=\"tex\">O A B C?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{85}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{90}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{95}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{105}</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{85}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{90}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{95}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">10</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{105}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>It may be assumed that <span class=\"tex\">A=(a, 0,0), B=(0, b, 0), C=(0,0, c)</span>, <span class=\"tex\">A B=5, B C=6</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">C A=7</span>. Then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">a^{2}+b^{2}=5^{2}, \\quad b^{2}+c^{2}=6^{2}, \\quad \\text { and } \\quad a^{2}+c^{2}=7^{2}</span></p><p>from which</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(5^{2}+6^{2}+7^{2}\\right)=55</span></p><p>It follows that <span class=\"tex\">a=\\sqrt{55-6^{2}}=\\sqrt{19}, b=\\sqrt{55-7^{2}}=\\sqrt{6}, c=\\sqrt{55-5^{2}}=\\sqrt{30}</span>, and the volume of tetrahedron <span class=\"tex\">O A B C</span> can be expressed as</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{1}{3} \\cdot O C \\cdot \\operatorname{Area}(\\triangle O A B)=\\dfrac{1}{6} \\sqrt{6 \\cdot 19 \\cdot 30}=\\boxed{\\sqrt{95}}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-18"}, {"q": "<p> In the expansion of</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left(1+x+x^{2}+\\cdots+x^{27}\\right)\\left(1+x+x^{2}+\\cdots+x^{14}\\right)^{2}</span></p><p>what is the coefficient of <span class=\"tex\">x^{28}?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">195</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">196</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">224</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">378</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">405</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">195</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">196</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">224</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">378</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">405</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Each term in the expansion has the form <span class=\"tex\">x^{a+b+c}</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq a \\leq 27,0 \\leq b \\leq 14</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq c \\leq 14</span>. There are <span class=\"tex\">(14+1)^{2}=225</span> possible combinations of values for <span class=\"tex\">b</span> and <span class=\"tex\">c</span>, and for every combination except <span class=\"tex\">(b, c)=(0,0)</span>, there is a unique <span class=\"tex\">a</span> with <span class=\"tex\">a+b+c=28</span>. Thus the coefficient of <span class=\"tex\">x^{28}</span> is \\boxed</p>\n<p>OR</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">P(x)=\\left(1+x+x^{2}+\\cdots+x^{14}\\right)^{2}=1+r_{1} x+r_{2} x^{2}+\\cdots+r_{28} x^{28}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">Q(x)=1+x+x^{2}+\\cdots+x^{27}</span>. The coefficient of <span class=\"tex\">x^{28}</span> in the product <span class=\"tex\">P(x) Q(x)</span> is <span class=\"tex\">r_{1}+r_{2}+\\cdots+r_{28}=P(1)-1=15^{2}-1= \\boxed{224}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-19"}, {"q": "<p> Triangle <span class=\"tex\">A B C</span> has <span class=\"tex\">A C=3, B C=4</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">A B=5</span>. Point <span class=\"tex\">D</span> is on <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A B}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span> bisects the right angle. The inscribed circles of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A D C</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B C D</span> have radii <span class=\"tex\">r_{a}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">r_{b}</span>, respectively. What is <span class=\"tex\">r_{a} / r_{b}?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{28}(10-\\sqrt{2})</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{56}(10-\\sqrt{2})</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{14}(10-\\sqrt{2})</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{56}(10-\\sqrt{2})</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{28}(10-\\sqrt{2})</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{28}(10-\\sqrt{2})</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{56}(10-\\sqrt{2})</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{14}(10-\\sqrt{2})</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{56}(10-\\sqrt{2})</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{28}(10-\\sqrt{2})</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>By the Angle Bisector Theorem,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">A D=5 \\cdot \\dfrac{3}{3+4}=\\dfrac{15}{7} \\quad \\text { and } \\quad B D=5 \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{3+4}=\\dfrac{20}{7}</span></p><p>To determine <span class=\"tex\">C D</span>, start with the relation Area <span class=\"tex\">(\\triangle A D C)+\\operatorname{Area}(\\triangle B C D)=</span> Area <span class=\"tex\">(\\triangle A B C)</span> to get</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{3 \\cdot C D}{2 \\sqrt{2}}+\\dfrac{4 \\cdot C D}{2 \\sqrt{2}}=\\dfrac{3 \\cdot 4}{2}</span></p><p>This gives <span class=\"tex\">C D=\\dfrac{12 \\sqrt{2}}{7}</span>. Now use the fact that the area of a triangle is given by <span class=\"tex\">r s</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">r</span> is the radius of the inscribed circle and <span class=\"tex\">s</span> is half the perimeter of the triangle. The ratio of the area of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A D C</span> to the area of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B C D</span> is the ratio of the altitudes to their common base <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span>, which is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{A D}{B D}=\\dfrac{3}{4}</span>. Hence</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{3}{4}=\\dfrac{\\operatorname{Area}(\\triangle A D C)}{\\operatorname{Area}(\\triangle B C D)}=\\dfrac{r_{a}\\left(3+\\dfrac{15}{7}+\\dfrac{12 \\sqrt{2}}{7}\\right)}{r_{b}\\left(4+\\dfrac{20}{7}+\\dfrac{12 \\sqrt{2}}{7}\\right)}</span></p><p>which yields</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{r_{a}}{r_{b}}=\\dfrac{3(4+\\sqrt{2})}{4(3+\\sqrt{2})}= \\boxed{\\dfrac{3}{28}(10-\\sqrt{2})}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-20"}, {"q": "<p> A permutation <span class=\"tex\">\\left(a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}, a_{4}, a_{5}\\right)</span> of <span class=\"tex\">(1,2,3,4,5)</span> is heavy-tailed if <span class=\"tex\">a_{1}+a_{2}&lt;a_{4}+a_{5}</span>. What is the number of heavy-tailed permutations?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">36</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">40</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">44</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">48</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">52</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">36</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">40</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">44</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">48</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">52</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Call a permutation balanced if <span class=\"tex\">a_{1}+a_{2}=a_{4}+a_{5}</span>, and consider the number of balanced permutations. The sum of all five entries is odd, so in a balanced permutation, <span class=\"tex\">a_{3}</span> must be 1,3 , or 5 . For each choice of <span class=\"tex\">a_{3}</span>, there is a unique way to group the remaining four numbers into two sets whose elements have equal sums. For example, if <span class=\"tex\">a_{3}=1</span>, the two sets must be <span class=\"tex\">\\{2,5\\}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\{3,4\\}</span>. Any one of the four numbers can be <span class=\"tex\">a_{1}</span>, and the value of <span class=\"tex\">a_{2}</span> is then determined. Either of the two remaining numbers can be <span class=\"tex\">a_{4}</span>, and the value of <span class=\"tex\">a_{5}</span> is then determined. Thus there are <span class=\"tex\">3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 4=24</span> balanced permutations of</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\">(1,2,3,4,5)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">5!-24=96</span> permutations that are not balanced. Call a permutation heavy-headed if <span class=\"tex\">a_{1}+a_{2}&gt;a_{4}+a_{5}</span>. Reversing the entries in a heavy-headed permutation yields a unique heavy-tailed permutation, and vice versa, so there are exactly as many heavy-headed permutations as heavy-tailed ones. Therefore the number of heavy-tailed permutations is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 96= \\boxed{48}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-21"}, {"q": "<p> A round table has radius <span class=\"tex\">4</span>. Six rectangular place mats are placed on the table. Each place mat has width <span class=\"tex\">1</span> and length <span class=\"tex\">x</span> as shown. They are positioned so that each mat has two corners on the edge of the table, these two corners being end points of the same side of length <span class=\"tex\">x</span>. Further, the mats are positioned so that the inner corners each touch an inner corner of an adjacent mat. What is <span class=\"tex\">x?</span></p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2008A/2023_11_14_bb2e7c9254f50af533a3g-114.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2 \\sqrt{5}-\\sqrt{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3 \\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3}}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2 \\sqrt{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5+2 \\sqrt{3}}{2}</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">2 \\sqrt{5}-\\sqrt{3}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">3</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3 \\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3}}{2}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">2 \\sqrt{3}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5+2 \\sqrt{3}}{2}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Select one of the mats. Let <span class=\"tex\">P</span> and <span class=\"tex\">Q</span> be the two corners of the mat that are on the edge of the table, and let <span class=\"tex\">R</span> be the point on the edge of the table that is diametrically opposite <span class=\"tex\">P</span> as shown. Then <span class=\"tex\">R</span> is also a corner of a mat and <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle P Q R</span> is a right triangle with hypotenuse <span class=\"tex\">P R=8</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">S</span> be the inner corner of the chosen mat that lies on <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{Q R}, T</span> the analogous point on the mat with corner <span class=\"tex\">R</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">U</span> the corner common</p>\n<center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2008A/2024_06_22_1fefda5e99366fb06e42g-143.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>\n<p>to the other mat with corner <span class=\"tex\">S</span> and the other mat with corner <span class=\"tex\">T</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle S T U</span> is an isosceles triangle with two sides of length <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and vertex angle <span class=\"tex\">120^{\\circ}</span>. It follows that <span class=\"tex\">S T=\\sqrt{3} x</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">Q R=Q S+S T+T R=\\sqrt{3} x+2</span>.</p>\n<p>Apply the Pythagorean Theorem to <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle P Q R</span> to obtain <span class=\"tex\">(\\sqrt{3} x+2)^{2}+x^{2}=8^{2}</span>, from which <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+\\sqrt{3} x-15=0</span>. Solve for <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and ignore the negative root to obtain</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\boxed{x=\\dfrac{3 \\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3}}{2}}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-22"}, {"q": "<p> The solutions of the equation <span class=\"tex\">z^{4}+4 z^{3} i-6 z^{2}-4 z i-i=0</span> are the vertices of a convex polygon in the complex plane. What is the area of the polygon?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2^{\\frac{5}{8}}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2^{\\frac{3}{4}}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2^{\\frac{5}{4}}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2^{\\frac{3}{2}}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">2^{\\frac{5}{8}}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">2^{\\frac{3}{4}}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">2</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">2^{\\frac{5}{4}}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">2^{\\frac{3}{2}}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Adding <span class=\"tex\">1+i</span> to each side of the given equation gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\">1+i=\\left(z^{4}+4 z^{3} i-6 z^{2}-4 z i-i\\right)+1+i=z^{4}+4 z^{3} i-6 z^{2}-4 z i+1=(z+i)^{4}</span>.</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">w=z+i=r(\\cos \\theta+i \\sin \\theta)</span>. Since</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">i+1=\\sqrt{2}\\left(\\cos \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+i \\sin \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)</span></p><p>the solutions of <span class=\"tex\">w^{4}=1+i</span> satisfy</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">r^{4}=\\sqrt{2} \\quad \\text { and } \\quad \\theta=\\dfrac{1}{4}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+2 k \\pi\\right)=\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} k</span></p><p>for <span class=\"tex\">k=0,1,2</span>, or 3 . Thus</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">w_{k}=2^{1 / 8}\\left(\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} k\\right)+i \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} k\\right)\\right) \\quad \\text { for } k=0,1,2, \\text { or } 3</span></p><p>and the four solutions for <span class=\"tex\">z=w-i</span> are</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">z_{k}=2^{1 / 8}\\left(\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} k\\right)+i \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} k\\right)\\right)-i \\quad \\text { for } k=0,1,2, \\text { or } 3</span></p><p>Note that <span class=\"tex\">w_{0}, w_{1}, w_{2}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">w_{3}</span> are equally spaced around the circle of radius <span class=\"tex\">2^{1 / 8}</span> centered at <span class=\"tex\">(0,0)</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">z_{0}, z_{1}, z_{2}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">z_{3}</span> are equally spaced around the circle of radius <span class=\"tex\">2^{1 / 8}</span> centered at <span class=\"tex\">(0,-1)</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">z_{0}, z_{1}, z_{2}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">z_{3}</span> are vertices of a square with side length <span class=\"tex\">2^{1 / 8} \\sqrt{2}=2^{5 / 8}</span> and area <span class=\"tex\">\\left(2^{5 / 8}\\right)^{2}= \\boxed{2^{5 / 4}}</span>.</p>\n<p>OR</p>\n<p>The Binomial Theorem gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(z+i)^{4}=z^{4}+4 z^{3} i-6 z^{2}-4 z i+1=\\left(z^{4}+4 z^{3} i-6 z^{2}-4 z i-i\\right)+1+i=1+i</span></p><p>Let <span class=\"tex\">a</span> satisfy <span class=\"tex\">a^{4}=1+i</span>, and let <span class=\"tex\">w=z+i</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">w^{4}=a^{4}</span>, so the possible values for <span class=\"tex\">w</span> are <span class=\"tex\">a, i a,-a</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">-i a</span>, which are the vertices of a square with diagonal <span class=\"tex\">2|a|=2 \\sqrt[8]{2}</span>. The transformation <span class=\"tex\">w=z+i</span> is a translation, so it preserves area. Hence the area of the original polygon is <span class=\"tex\">(2 \\sqrt[8]{2})^{2} / 2=2 \\sqrt[4]{2}=\\boxed{2^{5 / 4}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-23"}, {"q": "<p> Triangle <span class=\"tex\">A B C</span> has <span class=\"tex\">\\angle C=60^{\\circ}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B C=4</span>. Point <span class=\"tex\">D</span> is the midpoint of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{B C}</span>. What is the largest possible value of <span class=\"tex\">\\tan (\\angle B A D)?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2 \\sqrt{2}}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4 \\sqrt{2}-3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2 \\sqrt{2}}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4 \\sqrt{2}-3}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">1</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">C=(0,0), B=(2,2 \\sqrt{3})</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">A=(x, 0)</span> with <span class=\"tex\">x&gt;0</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">D=(1, \\sqrt{3})</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">P</span> be on the positive <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-axis to the right of <span class=\"tex\">A</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B A D=\\angle P A D-\\angle P A B</span>. Provided <span class=\"tex\">\\angle P A D</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\angle P A B</span> are not right angles, it follows that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (\\angle B A D) =\\tan (\\angle P A D-\\angle P A B)=\\dfrac{\\tan (\\angle P A D)-\\tan (\\angle P A B)}{1+\\tan (\\angle P A D) \\tan (\\angle P A B)} \\\\\n=\\dfrac{m_{A D}-m_{A B}}{1+m_{A D} m_{A B}}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{1-x}-\\dfrac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{2-x}}{1+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{1-x} \\cdot \\dfrac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{2-x}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3} x}{x^{2}-3 x+8} \\\\\n=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\left(\\sqrt{x}-\\dfrac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)^{2}+(4 \\sqrt{2}-3)} \\leq \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4 \\sqrt{2}-3},\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>with equality when <span class=\"tex\">x=2 \\sqrt{2}</span>. If <span class=\"tex\">\\angle P A D=90^{\\circ}</span>, then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\tan (\\angle B A D)=\\cot (\\angle P A B)=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\sqrt{3}}&lt;\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4 \\sqrt{2}-3}</span></p><p>If <span class=\"tex\">\\angle P A B=90^{\\circ}</span>, then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\tan (\\angle B A D)=-\\cot (\\angle P A D)=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}&lt;\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4 \\sqrt{2}-3}</span></p><p>Therefore the largest possible value of <span class=\"tex\">\\tan (\\angle B A D)</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{3} /(4 \\sqrt{2}-3)</span>.</p>\n<p>\\section*{OR}<br/>\nBecause the circle with diameter <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{B D}</span> does not intersect the line <span class=\"tex\">A C</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B A D&lt;90^{\\circ}</span>. Thus the value of <span class=\"tex\">\\tan (\\angle B A D)</span> is greatest when <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B A D</span> is greatest. This occurs when <span class=\"tex\">A</span> is placed to minimize the size of the circle passing through <span class=\"tex\">A, B</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">D</span>, so the maximum is attained when that circle is tangent to <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C}</span> at <span class=\"tex\">A</span>. For this location of <span class=\"tex\">A</span>, the Power of a Point Theorem implies that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">A C^{2}=C B \\cdot C D=4 \\cdot 2=8, \\text { and } A C=\\sqrt{8}=2 \\sqrt{2}</span></p><p>Because <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{C A}{C B}=\\dfrac{C D}{C A}</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle C A D</span> is similar to <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle C B A</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">A B=</span> <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{2} A D</span>. The Law of Cosines, applied to <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A D C</span>, gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">A D^{2}=C D^{2}+C A^{2}-2 C D \\cdot C A \\cdot \\cos 60^{\\circ}=12-4 \\sqrt{2}</span></p><p>Let <span class=\"tex\">O</span> be the center of the circle passing through <span class=\"tex\">A, B</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">D</span>. The Extended Law of Sines, applied to <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B D</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A D C</span>, gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n2 O B =\\dfrac{A B}{\\sin (\\angle B D A)}=\\dfrac{A B}{\\sin (\\angle A D C)} \\\\\n=\\dfrac{A B \\cdot A D}{A C \\cdot \\sin 60^{\\circ}}=\\dfrac{2 A B \\cdot A D}{\\sqrt{3} A C} \\\\\n=\\dfrac{2 \\sqrt{2} A D^{2}}{2 \\sqrt{2} \\sqrt{3}}=\\dfrac{A D^{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Let <span class=\"tex\">M</span> be the midpoint of <span class=\"tex\">B D</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B A D=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\angle B O D=\\angle B O M</span>, it follows that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (\\angle B A D) =\\tan (\\angle B O M)=\\dfrac{M B}{O M} \\\\\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{O B^{2}-1}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{A D^{4}}{12}-1}} \\\\\n=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{(6-2 \\sqrt{2})^{2}-3}}= \\boxed{\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4 \\sqrt{2}-3}}\n\\end{aligned}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-24"}, {"q": "<p> A sequence <span class=\"tex\">\\left(a_{1}, b_{1}\\right),\\left(a_{2}, b_{2}\\right),\\left(a_{3}, b_{3}\\right), \\ldots</span> of points in the coordinate plane satisfies</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left(a_{n+1}, b_{n+1}\\right)=\\left(\\sqrt{3} a_{n}-b_{n}, \\sqrt{3} b_{n}+a_{n}\\right) \\quad \\text { for } \\quad n=1,2,3, \\ldots</span></p><p>Suppose that <span class=\"tex\">\\left(a_{100}, b_{100}\\right)=(2,4)</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">a_{1}+b_{1}?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{1}{2^{97}}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{1}{2^{99}}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">0</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2^{98}}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2^{96}}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{1}{2^{97}}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{1}{2^{99}}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">0</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2^{98}}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2^{96}}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">z_{n}=a_{n}+b_{n} i</span>. Then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\nz_{n+1} =\\left(\\sqrt{3} a_{n}-b_{n}\\right)+\\left(\\sqrt{3} b_{n}+a_{n}\\right) i=\\left(a_{n}+b_{n} i\\right)(\\sqrt{3}+i) \\\\\n=z_{n}(\\sqrt{3}+i)=z_{1}(\\sqrt{3}+i)^{n}\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Noting that <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{3}+i=2\\left(\\cos \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}+i \\sin \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)</span> and applying DeMoivre's formula gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n2+4 i=z_{100} =z_{1}\\left(2\\left(\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)+i \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right)\\right)^{99} \\\\\n=z_{1} \\cdot 2^{99}\\left(\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{99 \\pi}{6}\\right)+i \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{99 \\pi}{6}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n=\\left(a_{1}+b_{1} i\\right) \\cdot 2^{99} \\cdot i=-2^{99} b_{1}+2^{99} a_{1} i\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>So <span class=\"tex\">2=-2^{99} b_{1}, 4=2^{99} a_{1}</span>, and</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">a_{1}+b_{1}=\\dfrac{4}{2^{99}}-\\dfrac{2}{2^{99}}= \\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{2^{98}}}</span></p><p>Note that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(a_{n+2}, b_{n+2}\\right)= \\left(\\sqrt{3}\\left(\\sqrt{3} a_{n}-b_{n}\\right)-\\left(\\sqrt{3} b_{n}+a_{n}\\right)\\right. \\\\\n\\left.\\sqrt{3}\\left(\\sqrt{3} b_{n}+a_{n}\\right)+\\left(\\sqrt{3} a_{n}-b_{n}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n= \\left(-2 \\sqrt{3} b_{n}+2 a_{n}, 2 \\sqrt{3} a_{n}+2 b_{n}\\right) \\\\\n\\left(a_{n+3}, b_{n+3}\\right)= \\left(\\sqrt{3}\\left(-2 \\sqrt{3} b_{n}+2 a_{n}\\right)-\\left(2 \\sqrt{3} a_{n}+2 b_{n}\\right)\\right. \\\\\n\\left.\\sqrt{3}\\left(2 \\sqrt{3} a_{n}+2 b_{n}\\right)+\\left(-2 \\sqrt{3} b_{n}+2 a_{n}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n= 8\\left(-b_{n}, a_{n}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>and <span class=\"tex\">\\left(a_{n+6}, b_{n+6}\\right)=8\\left(-b_{n+3}, a_{n+3}\\right)=-64\\left(a_{n}, b_{n}\\right)</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">97=1+16 \\cdot 6</span>, we have</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left(a_{97}, b_{97}\\right)=(-64)^{16}\\left(a_{1}, b_{1}\\right)=2^{96}\\left(a_{1}, b_{1}\\right)</span></p><p>and</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(2,4)=\\left(a_{100}, b_{100}\\right)=2^{3}\\left(-b_{97}, a_{97}\\right)=2^{99}\\left(-b_{1}, a_{1}\\right)</span></p><p>The conclusion follows as in the first solution.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2008/part-a/problem-25"}]});
